  storyEn: "In the fifth century, when the very idea of a university had not yet dawned in most of the world, Nalanda Mahavihara on this soil of Bihar had already become a centre of learning whose fame stretched from China to Tibet and Korea. The Gupta emperor Kumaragupta I founded it, and later emperors such as Harshavardhana endowed it generously. At its height, nearly ten thousand students and two thousand teachers lived and studied here together — the largest residential university of its age. Admission was fiercely difficult; learned scholars seated at the gates examined every applicant, and it is said that seven or eight out of ten aspirants were turned away at the door itself. The curriculum went far beyond Buddhist philosophy — logic, medicine, astronomy, grammar and metaphysics were all taught here. The Chinese pilgrim Xuanzang arrived in 637 CE and spent two years studying Yogacara philosophy under the master Shilabhadra; his travel records describe in detail the discipline, the libraries and the open debating tradition of Nalanda scholars. Later pilgrims such as Yijing studied here too. The great library — Ratnasagara, Ratnodadhi and Ratnaranjaka — was a complex of nine-storeyed buildings holding hundreds of thousands of manuscripts. Legend holds that when Nalanda was destroyed around 1193 CE during the invasions of Bakhtiyar Khilji, the library burned for months. That glory lay buried in earth for centuries until archaeologists excavated it in the 19th and 20th centuries. Today the ruins of monasteries, temples, stupas and prayer halls built of red brick stretch far and wide. The nearby Nalanda Archaeological Museum displays excavated Buddha images, coins and manuscripts, and the Xuanzang Memorial Hall honours the Chinese pilgrim. In 2016 Nalanda was declared a UNESCO World Heritage Site, and in 2014 a new Nalanda University was established close by — a modern rebirth of the ancient tradition of learning. Among Nalanda\u2019s masters were the logician Dharmakirti, the Madhyamika philosopher Chandrakirti and Shantarakshita, whose works remain the foundation of Tibetan Buddhist education. Clay sealings unearthed here bear the legend \u201cSri-Nalanda-Mahavihara\u201d — the university\u2019s official seal. Walking among these ruins today, the prayer-hall steps, monastery courtyards and rows of stupas echo an age when the sounding of the great bell gathered thousands of students for debate.",
